-- fuvahmulah
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Gnaviyani Atoll, Southern Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Single-Island Atoll & Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 12,000 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"4.9 km long, 1.2 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"495 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Domestic flight (1 hour 20 minutes)"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Tiger shark diving, freshwater lakes, unique geography"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Divers, nature enthusiasts, adventure travelers"}],"island_overview":["Fuvahmulah Island is truly one of the most unique destinations in the Maldives, being the only single-island atoll in the country. This extraordinary island stands apart with its distinctive geography, featuring freshwater lakes, diverse ecosystems, and geological formations found nowhere else in the Maldives.","Located in Gnaviyani Atoll in the southern Maldives, Fuvahmulah is renowned worldwide among divers for its incredible tiger shark encounters and unique marine biodiversity. The island''s position in deep oceanic waters creates exceptional diving conditions and attracts pelagic species rarely seen elsewhere.","What makes Fuvahmulah truly remarkable is its combination of terrestrial uniqueness and marine excellence. From freshwater lakes and wetlands to world-class diving with tiger sharks, thresher sharks, and other pelagic species, this island offers experiences found nowhere else in the Maldives."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Fuvahmulah Island is located in the far south of the Maldives, approximately 495 kilometers from Male. As the only single-island atoll in the Maldives, it sits in deep oceanic waters, creating unique conditions for both terrestrial and marine ecosystems.","The island''s isolated position in the Indian Ocean contributes to its distinctive characteristics, including its freshwater lakes, unique vegetation, and exceptional marine biodiversity. Its location attracts pelagic species and creates some of the best diving conditions in the Maldives."]},{"heading":"Fuvahmulah: Unique Geography & Marine Wonders","paragraphs":["Fuvahmulah Island stands as a geological and biological marvel in the Maldives, offering features found nowhere else in the archipelago. From freshwater lakes to world-class tiger shark diving, this island provides extraordinary experiences for nature lovers and adventure seekers."]},{"heading":"Freshwater Lakes & Wetlands","paragraphs":["Fuvahmulah is the only island in the Maldives with natural freshwater lakes, creating unique ecosystems and supporting diverse flora and fauna."]},{"heading":"World-Class Tiger Shark Diving","paragraphs":["Fuvahmulah is globally renowned for its tiger shark encounters, offering some of the most reliable tiger shark diving experiences in the world."]},{"heading":"Extraordinary Biodiversity","paragraphs":["The island''s unique position and geography support diverse ecosystems, from wetland birds to endemic plant species not found elsewhere in the Maldives."]},{"heading":"Distinctive Geological Features","paragraphs":["As a single-island atoll, Fuvahmulah displays unique geological formations, elevated areas, and diverse coastal environments."]},{"heading":"Unique Natural Features","paragraphs":["Fuvahmulah''s distinctive characteristics include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The waters around Fuvahmulah feature:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Fuvahmulah offers unparalleled activities combining world-class diving with unique terrestrial experiences. The island''s distinctive features provide opportunities for adventure, nature exploration, and marine encounters found nowhere else in the Maldives."]},{"heading":"Tiger Shark Diving","paragraphs":["Experience world-renowned tiger shark encounters in the deep waters around Fuvahmulah, with year-round sightings and exceptional visibility."]},{"heading":"Freshwater Lake Exploration","paragraphs":["Visit the unique freshwater lakes and wetlands, the only ones in the Maldives, supporting diverse bird life and endemic species."]},{"heading":"Pelagic Marine Encounters","paragraphs":["Encounter thresher sharks, hammerheads, whale sharks, and manta rays in the deep oceanic waters surrounding the island."]},{"heading":"Bird Watching & Nature Tours","paragraphs":["Observe migratory birds, endemic species, and diverse wildlife in the island''s unique wetland and terrestrial ecosystems."]},{"heading":"Cultural & Historical Tours","paragraphs":["Explore the island''s rich history, traditional culture, and unique community life in this distinctive Maldivian setting."]},{"heading":"Photography & Research","paragraphs":["Capture unique landscapes, marine life, and biodiversity while contributing to conservation and research efforts."]},{"heading":"Community & Conservation","paragraphs":["Fuvahmulah is home to approximately 12,000 residents, making it one of the most populous islands in the Maldives. The community has adapted to the island''s unique environment and plays a crucial role in conservation efforts to protect its distinctive ecosystems."]},{"heading":"Conservation Efforts","paragraphs":["The island''s unique biodiversity and marine environment require special conservation measures. Local communities, researchers, and conservation organizations work together to protect the freshwater lakes, wetlands, and marine ecosystems."]},{"heading":"Research & Education","paragraphs":["Fuvahmulah serves as an important research site for marine biology, ecology, and climate studies. The island''s unique characteristics provide valuable insights into biodiversity, conservation, and sustainable development."]},{"heading":"Sustainable Tourism","paragraphs":["The community promotes responsible tourism practices that protect the island''s unique environment while providing economic benefits to local residents through diving, accommodation, and guide services."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Fuvahmulah Island?","answer":"Fuvahmulah is accessible by domestic flight from Male (1 hour 20 minutes) via Maldivian airlines. Regular flights operate daily, and the island has its own airport. Seaplane transfers are limited due to the distance and weather conditions."},{"question":"What makes Fuvahmulah unique compared to other Maldivian islands?","answer":"Fuvahmulah is the only single-island atoll in the Maldives with natural freshwater lakes, unique geology, and exceptional marine biodiversity. It''s world-renowned for tiger shark diving and offers terrestrial experiences found nowhere else in the Maldives."},{"question":"When is the best time for tiger shark diving?","answer":"Tiger sharks can be encountered year-round at Fuvahmulah, but the best conditions are typically from December to April during the dry season. However, the island''s unique location provides excellent diving opportunities throughout the year."},{"question":"Can I visit the freshwater lakes?","answer":"Yes, guided tours to the freshwater lakes (Dhadimagi Kilhi and Bandaara Kilhi) are available. These tours provide insights into the unique ecosystems and conservation efforts while respecting the sensitive environmental areas."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["gemanafushi","kanduhulhudhoo"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'fuvahmulah';


-- gaadhiffushi
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Thaa Atoll, Central Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 500 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"0.6 km long, 0.3 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"240 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Domestic flight + speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Pristine beaches, excellent snorkeling, peaceful atmosphere"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Nature lovers, snorkelers, cultural travelers, budget travelers"}],"island_overview":["Nestled in the pristine waters of Thaa Atoll, Gaadhiffushi Island represents the essence of authentic Maldivian island life. This charming local island offers visitors a unique opportunity to experience traditional culture while enjoying some of the most beautiful beaches in the central Maldives.","Gaadhiffushi stands out for its untouched natural beauty, crystal-clear lagoons, and vibrant marine life. The island maintains its traditional character while welcoming visitors with genuine Maldivian hospitality and warmth.","What makes Gaadhiffushi truly special is its peaceful atmosphere, excellent marine life, and the opportunity to experience authentic island culture. Visitors can enjoy budget-friendly accommodations, delicious local cuisine, and unforgettable underwater adventures."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Gaadhiffushi Island is located in the central part of Thaa Atoll, approximately 240 kilometers from Male. This remote location ensures an authentic and peaceful experience, far from the bustling tourist areas, while still offering excellent accessibility.","The island''s position provides natural protection from strong currents while maintaining access to some of the best snorkeling and diving sites in the Maldives. The surrounding waters feature healthy coral reefs and abundant marine life."]},{"heading":"Gaadhiffushi: Natural Beauty & Marine Wonders","paragraphs":["Gaadhiffushi Island is renowned for its exceptional natural beauty, featuring powder-white sand beaches, crystal-clear turquoise waters, and vibrant marine life. The island offers an unspoiled tropical paradise perfect for relaxation and marine exploration."]},{"heading":"Pristine White Sand Beaches","paragraphs":["Gaadhiffushi boasts some of the most beautiful beaches in Thaa Atoll, with soft white sand and crystal-clear waters perfect for swimming and sunbathing."]},{"heading":"World-Class Snorkeling","paragraphs":["The island''s house reef features diverse marine life, colorful corals, and excellent underwater visibility for memorable snorkeling experiences."]},{"heading":"Authentic Island Culture","paragraphs":["Experience genuine Maldivian hospitality and traditional island life in a peaceful setting away from commercial tourism."]},{"heading":"Pristine Sandbank Visits","paragraphs":["Explore nearby uninhabited sandbanks for the ultimate desert island experience with picnics and swimming in crystal-clear waters."]},{"heading":"Beach Highlights","paragraphs":["Gaadhiffushi''s coastal features include:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The island''s underwater world features:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Gaadhiffushi offers an excellent variety of activities focused on marine adventures, cultural experiences, and natural exploration. The island''s pristine environment provides perfect opportunities for both adventure and relaxation."]},{"heading":"House Reef Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the vibrant house reef directly from the beach, featuring colorful corals, tropical fish, and excellent underwater visibility."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Join local fishermen for authentic fishing experiences using traditional techniques and learn about the island''s fishing heritage."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping Tours","paragraphs":["Visit nearby islands and discover uninhabited sandbanks, each offering unique experiences and pristine natural beauty."]},{"heading":"Cultural Experiences","paragraphs":["Immerse yourself in local community life, learn traditional crafts, and experience authentic Maldivian customs and traditions."]},{"heading":"Sunset Cruises","paragraphs":["Enjoy magical sunset cruises around the atoll, often combined with dolphin watching and traditional dhoni sailing."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving Adventures","paragraphs":["Discover world-class dive sites around Thaa Atoll with professional dive operators offering courses and guided dives."]},{"heading":"People & Community Life","paragraphs":["Gaadhiffushi is home to a close-knit community of approximately 500 residents who have preserved traditional Maldivian ways of life for generations. The island''s population is renowned for their warmth, hospitality, and strong community bonds."]},{"heading":"Daily Life","paragraphs":["Life on Gaadhiffushi follows the natural rhythms of island living, beginning with morning prayers and continuing with fishing, community work, and family activities. The island maintains a peaceful, unhurried pace that reflects traditional Maldivian values."]},{"heading":"Culture & Traditions","paragraphs":["Gaadhiffushi preserves authentic Maldivian cultural traditions including traditional music, dance, and handicrafts. The community celebrates Islamic festivals with enthusiasm and maintains traditional practices such as dhoni building and traditional fishing techniques."]},{"heading":"Local Economy","paragraphs":["The island''s economy is primarily based on fishing and increasingly tourism. Many families engage in traditional occupations while also participating in the growing tourism sector through guesthouses, restaurants, and tour services."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Gaadhiffushi Island?","answer":"Gaadhiffushi Island can be reached by domestic flight to Thimarafushi Airport (1.5 hours from Male) followed by a 20-minute speedboat ride. Seaplane transfers are also available but depend on weather conditions. There''s also a weekly public ferry from Male that takes 14-16 hours."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Gaadhiffushi?","answer":"The best time to visit Gaadhiffushi is during the dry season from December to April when weather conditions are most favorable. However, the island can be visited year-round, with the wet season (May-November) offering fewer crowds and lower accommodation rates."},{"question":"What accommodation options are available on Gaadhiffushi?","answer":"Gaadhiffushi offers various budget-friendly and mid-range accommodations including family guesthouses and beachfront properties. Most accommodations offer air conditioning, private bathrooms, and meal packages."},{"question":"What activities are available on Gaadhiffushi?","answer":"Gaadhiffushi offers excellent snorkeling from the house reef, diving excursions, island hopping tours, cultural experiences, traditional fishing trips, and sunset cruises. The island is particularly known for its pristine marine environment and authentic cultural experiences."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["guraidhoo","dhiyamigili","burunee","vilufushi","veymandoo","vandhoo","thimarafushi","omadhoo","madifushi","kinbidhoo","kandoodhoo","hirilandhoo"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'gaadhiffushi';


-- gaafaru
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Kaafu Atoll, North Male Atoll"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 1,200 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"1 km long, 0.5 km wide"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"35 km"},{"label":"Transfer Options","value":"Public ferry, speedboat"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Traditional fishing village, pristine beaches, excellent diving"},{"label":"Best For","value":"Cultural experiences, diving, authentic island life"}],"island_overview":["Located in the Kaafu Atoll northeast of Male, Gaafaru Island offers visitors an authentic Maldivian fishing village experience with a strong sense of community and traditional culture. This peaceful island is known for its laid-back atmosphere and stunning natural beauty, making it an ideal destination for travelers seeking an off-the-beaten-path experience.","What makes Gaafaru special is its preservation of traditional Maldivian fishing practices and way of life. The island''s economy remains largely based on fishing, and visitors can observe the daily rhythms of this essential Maldivian industry. The surrounding waters feature vibrant coral reefs and abundant marine life, making it a great spot for snorkeling and diving enthusiasts.","Visitors to Gaafaru are drawn by its pristine beaches, excellent diving opportunities, and chances to engage with local community life. The island''s relatively close proximity to Male means easier access than more remote islands, while still offering an authentic experience away from the tourist crowds."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Gaafaru Island is situated in the northeastern part of Kaafu Atoll (North Male Atoll), approximately 35 kilometers from Male. This location provides relatively easy access while maintaining a peaceful, traditional atmosphere away from the capital''s urban environment.","Its position in the atoll offers access to excellent dive sites with vibrant coral reefs and abundant marine life. The surrounding waters are particularly known for their clear visibility and diverse underwater ecosystems."]},{"heading":"Gaafaru: Traditional Fishing Village & Diving Paradise","paragraphs":["Gaafaru Island stands out among Maldivian islands for its strong fishing heritage and excellent diving opportunities, offering visitors a distinctive blend of cultural immersion and marine adventures."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing Village","paragraphs":["Gaafaru maintains its identity as a working fishing village, where visitors can observe traditional fishing techniques and the daily rhythms of island life centered around the sea."]},{"heading":"Excellent Diving","paragraphs":["The waters around Gaafaru feature several excellent dive sites with healthy coral reefs, abundant marine life, and good visibility, making it a favorite for diving enthusiasts."]},{"heading":"Natural Beauty","paragraphs":["Gaafaru boasts pristine white sand beaches with crystal-clear waters, offering the classic Maldivian beach experience with a peaceful, uncrowded atmosphere."]},{"heading":"Island Community","paragraphs":["Gaafaru is home to a tight-knit community that preserves traditional Maldivian values, offering visitors authentic cultural interactions in a welcoming environment."]},{"heading":"Fishing Village Life","paragraphs":["Gaafaru''s fishing heritage includes several distinctive elements:"]},{"heading":"Diving Highlights","paragraphs":["Gaafaru''s underwater environment offers:"]},{"heading":"Beaches & Marine Life","paragraphs":["Gaafaru is blessed with beautiful beaches and a spectacular marine environment. The island''s location in North Male Atoll provides access to healthy coral reefs and abundant marine life, offering excellent snorkeling and diving opportunities."]},{"heading":"Beautiful Beaches","paragraphs":["Gaafaru features several stunning beaches with soft white sand and crystal-clear turquoise waters. The main beach on the eastern side offers beautiful views and good swimming conditions. The northern beach is designated for tourists and allows Western-style swimwear, providing a relaxing environment while respecting local cultural norms."]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The waters around Gaafaru Island offer excellent marine biodiversity with healthy fringing reefs accessible directly from shore. Regular sightings include reef sharks, turtles, and colorful reef fish. The clear waters provide good visibility for snorkeling and diving."]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Gaafaru offers a range of activities centered around its fishing culture and marine environment, providing visitors with options for both cultural immersion and ocean adventures."]},{"heading":"Fishing Experiences","paragraphs":["Join local fishermen for traditional fishing trips using pole-and-line methods or try your hand at night fishing under the stars."]},{"heading":"Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore vibrant house reefs directly from shore or join boat excursions to nearby reef systems teeming with marine life."]},{"heading":"Diving","paragraphs":["Discover excellent dive sites around Gaafaru with healthy coral formations and abundant marine species."]},{"heading":"Sandbank Excursions","paragraphs":["Visit pristine uninhabited sandbanks for picnics, swimming, and the ultimate desert island experience."]},{"heading":"Island Hopping","paragraphs":["Visit neighboring islands in Kaafu Atoll to experience different communities and environments."]},{"heading":"Cultural Experiences","paragraphs":["Learn about traditional Maldivian life through cooking demonstrations, craft workshops, and community interactions."]},{"heading":"People & Community Life","paragraphs":["Gaafaru is home to a close-knit community of approximately 1,200 residents who maintain traditional values and way of life. The island''s population is known for its warm hospitality and preservation of authentic Maldivian culture."]},{"heading":"Daily Life","paragraphs":["Daily life on Gaafaru revolves around fishing and family. The island awakens before dawn as fishermen prepare their boats and gear. Women often work in fish processing or homemaking, while children attend the local school. Evenings are spent socializing, playing sports, or relaxing by the beach."]},{"heading":"Culture & Traditions","paragraphs":["Gaafaru preserves many traditional Maldivian customs and cultural practices. Religious observances play an important role in community life, with festivals like Eid celebrated enthusiastically. Cultural expressions such as Boduberu performances remain vibrant, while traditional fishing methods and local cuisine are still maintained."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Gaafaru Island?","answer":"Gaafaru is accessible by public ferry from Male, which operates several times a week and takes about 2 hours. Speedboat transfers are also available daily from Male or directly from Velana International Airport, taking approximately 45 minutes. Most guesthouses can arrange these transfers for you."},{"question":"What is there to do on Gaafaru Island?","answer":"Gaafaru offers cultural experiences like fishing trips with locals, traditional craft demonstrations, and village tours. The excellent diving and snorkeling opportunities are major draws, along with relaxing on pristine beaches and visiting nearby sandbanks."},{"question":"Are there restaurants on Gaafaru Island?","answer":"Gaafaru has a few small local cafes serving Maldivian cuisine. Most visitors eat at their guesthouses, which provide home-cooked meals featuring fresh local ingredients, especially seafood. Some guesthouses can accommodate special dietary requests with advance notice."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["himmafushi","huraa","dhiffushi","maafushi","gulhi","guraidhoo","hulhumale","male","kaashidhoo"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'gaafaru';


-- gaddhoo
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Faresmaathodaa Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 18-20 hours journey time). Private speedboat transfers can be arranged through guesthouses (approximately 7.5 hours from Male)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Faresmaathodaa Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Faresmaathodaa Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. For whale shark encounters, visit during the southwest monsoon (May to December). Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year."},{"question":"What makes Faresmaathodaa different from other local islands in the Maldives?","answer":"Faresmaathodaa stands out for its exceptional preservation of traditional Maldivian life in a remote setting and its unique twin-island structure. Unlike most Maldivian islands, Faresmaathodaa consists of two separate islands connected by a small bridge, creating a distinctive figure-eight shape. This unique geography provides additional shoreline and beach access, while the channel between the islands creates a natural harbor and a special marine environment."},{"question":"Can I walk between the two parts of Faresmaathodaa Island?","answer":"Yes, the two parts of Faresmaathodaa Island are connected by a small bridge that allows easy pedestrian access between them. Walking across this bridge is actually a popular activity for visitors, as it offers excellent views of the channel and surrounding waters. The bridge has become a gathering spot for locals and visitors alike, especially during sunset when the views are particularly spectacular."},{"question":"What marine life can I expect to see while snorkeling around Faresmaathodaa?","answer":"Faresmaathodaa''s waters offer excellent marine biodiversity, including healthy coral formations with both hard and soft corals. Common sightings include reef sharks (particularly blacktip and whitetip), sea turtles, various ray species, and abundant reef fish. The channel between the two parts of the island creates a unique marine environment with different currents and marine life. Seasonal visitors include whale sharks (May-December) and manta rays. The house reef provides easy access to this underwater world directly from shore."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'gaddhoo';


-- gan
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[{"label":"Location","value":"Laamu Atoll, Southern Maldives"},{"label":"Island Type","value":"Inhabited Local Island"},{"label":"Population","value":"Approximately 2,500 residents"},{"label":"Size","value":"5.8 km long (largest in Laamu Atoll)"},{"label":"Distance from Male","value":"235 km"},{"label":"Known For","value":"Historic sites, long beaches, diving"},{"label":"Best For","value":"History buffs, divers, beach lovers"}],"island_overview":["Located in the southern Laamu Atoll, Gan Island is the largest island in the atoll with a rich history and stunning natural beauty. This unique island combines historical significance with beautiful beaches and excellent diving opportunities.","What makes Gan special is its fascinating history as a British naval base during World War II, combined with its natural attractions. The island features one of the longest beaches in the Maldives, historical ruins, and access to some of the best diving sites in Laamu Atoll.","Visitors to Gan are drawn by its historical sites, beautiful beaches, excellent diving, and authentic local culture. The island''s airport provides convenient access while maintaining an authentic Maldivian atmosphere."],"island_sections":[{"heading":"Location & Map","paragraphs":["Gan Island is situated in the southern part of Laamu Atoll, approximately 235 kilometers from Male. The island''s strategic location made it important during World War II, and today it serves as the transportation hub for Laamu Atoll with its domestic airport.","The island''s position offers excellent access to diving sites and nearby islands. The surrounding waters feature healthy coral reefs and abundant marine life, making it a great destination for underwater exploration."]},{"heading":"Gan: Beaches & Natural Beauty","paragraphs":["Gan Island is renowned for its long beaches and natural beauty, featuring one of the longest stretches of beach in the Maldives. The island offers visitors a combination of historical interest and tropical paradise."]},{"heading":"Long Sandy Beaches","paragraphs":["The island features stunning long stretches of white sand beach with crystal-clear turquoise waters, perfect for long walks and relaxation."]},{"heading":"Excellent Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore vibrant coral reefs accessible from shore, featuring diverse marine life and clear waters perfect for snorkeling."]},{"heading":"Authentic Island Life","paragraphs":["Experience traditional Maldivian culture and hospitality in a setting that balances modern amenities with authentic charm."]},{"heading":"Historical Significance","paragraphs":["Discover remnants of British military presence during WWII, including old bunkers and airstrips now used for civilian flights."]},{"heading":"Beach Highlights","paragraphs":["Gan''s beaches offer:"]},{"heading":"Marine Environment","paragraphs":["The island''s marine ecosystem features:"]},{"heading":"Activities & Experiences","paragraphs":["Gan offers a variety of activities focused on history, marine adventures, and cultural experiences. The island''s unique combination of attractions provides something for every type of traveler."]},{"heading":"Historical Exploration","paragraphs":["Discover remnants of the British military presence including old bunkers, airstrips, and other WWII relics."]},{"heading":"Snorkeling","paragraphs":["Explore the vibrant house reef directly from shore or join boat excursions to nearby coral gardens."]},{"heading":"Scuba Diving","paragraphs":["Discover excellent dive sites with professional operators offering courses and guided dives."]},{"heading":"Sandbank Visits","paragraphs":["Experience pristine uninhabited sandbanks for picnics and swimming in crystal-clear waters."]},{"heading":"Traditional Fishing","paragraphs":["Join local fishermen for traditional night fishing experiences under the stars."]},{"heading":"Beach Relaxation","paragraphs":["Enjoy the island''s long stretches of pristine beach, perfect for sunbathing and leisurely walks."]},{"heading":"People & Community Life","paragraphs":["Gan is home to a welcoming community of approximately 2,500 residents who balance traditional Maldivian life with modern influences. The island''s history as a military base has given it a unique cultural blend."]},{"heading":"Daily Life","paragraphs":["Life on Gan follows traditional Maldivian rhythms while incorporating modern conveniences. Fishing remains important, along with tourism-related work and services supporting the airport. The community is known for being particularly welcoming to visitors."]},{"heading":"Culture & Traditions","paragraphs":["Gan preserves authentic Maldivian cultural traditions while also reflecting its unique history. The island celebrates Islamic festivals with enthusiasm and maintains traditional practices such as boat building, fishing techniques, and local crafts."]},{"heading":"Local Economy","paragraphs":["The island''s economy is based on fishing, tourism, and airport services. Many families engage in traditional occupations while also participating in the tourism sector through guesthouses, restaurants, and tour services."]}],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Gan Island?","answer":"Some resorts offer seaplane transfers (seasonal)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Gan?","answer":"The best time is during the dry season (December-April) with sunny weather. However, the island can be visited year-round, with the wet season (May-November) offering fewer crowds."},{"question":"What makes Gan Island unique?","answer":"Gan is unique for its WWII history, being the largest island in Laamu Atoll, and having one of the longest beaches in the Maldives. Its airport also makes it the transportation hub for the atoll."},{"question":"What historical sites can I see on Gan?","answer":"You can see remnants of the British military base including old bunkers, the original airstrip (now the airport), storage buildings, and other WWII-era structures scattered around the island."}],"island_nearby_slugs":["fonadhoo","maabaidhoo","mundoo","isdhoo","kunahandhoo","dhanbidhoo","hithadhoo","maamendhoo","maavah"]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'gan';


-- gemanafushi
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Gemanafushi Island?","answer":"Due to the island''s remote location in the southern atolls, direct public ferry services from Male are limited. Most guesthouses can arrange the complete transfer from Male, including the domestic flight and speedboat connection. When planning your journey, remember that weather conditions can occasionally affect flight schedules in this region, so it''s advisable to allow buffer days in your travel plans. The journey to Gemanafushi is part of the adventure, offering beautiful aerial views of the Maldivian atolls during the flight and a chance to experience the stunning blue waters during the speedboat transfer."},{"question":"What marine conservation activities can visitors participate in on Gemanafushi?","answer":"Gemanafushi offers several ways for visitors to engage with and contribute to marine conservation efforts. Coral restoration experiences allow participants to learn about and assist with coral nursery maintenance and transplantation projects under the guidance of local conservation teams. Educational snorkeling tours with knowledgeable guides provide insights into reef ecosystems, marine species identification, and conservation challenges while observing the underwater environment firsthand. Beach and reef clean-up activities are regularly organized, giving visitors the opportunity to help remove marine debris and plastics from coastal areas. Marine conservation workshops and presentations offer deeper understanding of local and global marine conservation issues, often led by community conservation leaders. Some guesthouses also offer citizen science opportunities where visitors can help collect data on reef health, marine species populations, or water quality that contributes to ongoing monitoring efforts. The level of involvement can be adjusted based on your interest, time, and experience, from simple observation to active participation. These activities not only provide meaningful experiences but also directly contribute to the community''s conservation efforts."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Gemanafushi Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Gemanafushi Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas. This period is ideal for water activities, with excellent visibility for snorkeling and diving, often exceeding 30 meters. For those interested in marine conservation activities, this season offers more stable conditions for coral restoration work and reef monitoring. The southwest monsoon (May to November) brings occasional rain showers but also lower prices and fewer tourists. Water temperature remains warm (27-30C/80-86F) throughout the year. Due to Gemanafushi''s southern location, it may experience slightly different weather patterns than northern atolls, with the possibility of brief rain showers even during the dry season. The shoulder months of November and April often offer a good balance of favorable weather and lower visitor numbers. For specific marine life encounters, different seasons may be preferable - manta rays are more commonly seen during the southwest monsoon when plankton levels are higher, while overall reef visibility is better during the northeast monsoon."},{"question":"What type of accommodation can I expect on Gemanafushi?","answer":"Accommodation on Gemanafushi ranges from simple guesthouses to more comfortable boutique properties, reflecting the island''s growing but still authentic tourism development. The island offers several family-run guesthouses that provide clean, comfortable rooms with basic amenities including air conditioning, private bathrooms with hot water, and Wi-Fi (though connectivity may vary). Many accommodations on Gemanafushi have adopted eco-friendly practices such as water conservation, waste reduction, and energy efficiency measures. Some properties specifically market themselves as eco-guesthouses, with features like solar power, rainwater harvesting, or plastic-free operations. Most accommodations offer full-board packages that include three home-cooked Maldivian meals daily, featuring sustainable seafood caught by local fishermen. A few higher-end boutique properties provide enhanced amenities such as more spacious rooms, better furnishings, and additional services while still maintaining an authentic island experience. Several properties are located along the beachfront, offering direct access to the island''s beautiful shores. What makes Gemanafushi''s accommodations distinctive is their connection to conservation initiatives - many guesthouses actively participate in or support local marine conservation efforts, and some donate a portion of their proceeds to these projects. Visitors should still adjust their expectations to island standards rather than expecting resort-style luxury, embracing the authentic experience and conservation focus that makes staying on Gemanafushi special."},{"question":"How is Gemanafushi''s marine environment different from other Maldivian islands?","answer":"Gemanafushi''s marine environment stands out for several reasons that make it particularly special among Maldivian islands. First, its location in the southern Gaafu Alifu Atoll means it benefits from less tourism development and fishing pressure compared to atolls closer to Male, resulting in healthier reef systems with greater coral cover and fish biomass. The community''s proactive conservation initiatives have created protected areas where marine life can thrive with minimal human impact, leading to more abundant and diverse ecosystems than on islands without such protections. Coral restoration projects have helped rehabilitate damaged reef areas, creating new habitats and strengthening ecosystem resilience against climate threats. The island''s conservation focus has fostered a culture of environmental awareness among both residents and visitors, reducing destructive practices and promoting sustainable interactions with marine environments. Gemanafushi''s reefs feature exceptional biodiversity with over 250 coral species and abundant marine life ranging from tiny reef fish to larger pelagic species and sharks. The community''s monitoring programs provide ongoing data about reef health, allowing for adaptive management approaches that respond to changing conditions. Educational components integrated into marine activities create more informed and responsible visitors who better understand and appreciate the underwater environment. These factors combine to create a marine experience that not only showcases the natural beauty of Maldivian reefs but also demonstrates how community-led conservation can help preserve these fragile ecosystems for future generations."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
where node_type = 'location' and slug = 'gemanafushi';


-- goidhoo-baa
update nodes set attributes = coalesce(attributes, '{}'::jsonb) || '{"island_content_source":"legacy_mtg_guide","island_quick_facts":[],"island_overview":[],"island_sections":[],"island_faqs":[{"question":"How do I get to Goidhoo Island?","answer":"Public ferry service operates weekly from Male (approximately 12 hours journey time)."},{"question":"What is the best time to visit Goidhoo Island?","answer":"The best time to visit Goidhoo Island is during the northeast monsoon season (December to April), which brings dry, sunny weather with lower humidity and calmer seas."}],"island_nearby_slugs":[]}'::jsonb
